/* API keys as key pairs (ARCHITECTURE.md section 11): the SDK holds the
   private half and signs each request; the server stores only the public
   half, so no bearer secret crosses the network.

     Authorization: Runtime-Ed25519 Credential=<32 hex>,Timestamp=<ns>,Nonce=<32 hex>,Signature=<128 hex>
     Runtime-Project: <32 hex>

   The signature is Ed25519 over
     "rtr\x01" | credential (16, LE) | project (16, LE) | timestamp ns (8, LE)
     | nonce (16) | SHA-256(body) (32) | method | "\n" | path and query | "\n"
     | Idempotency-Key (empty when none)
   so it is good for one request, on one project, within 60 seconds of the
   server's clock, and a changed byte of the body, path, query or key refuses
   it. A signed write must carry an Idempotency-Key: a copy replayed within
   the minute is then answered from the first one's commit, not run again.
   The front's side is engines/front/crates/front/src/signed.rs, and every
   SDK signs the same test vector to the same header.

   In Node and Bun, node:crypto signs synchronously (15 µs in Bun, 36 µs in
   Node 24, on 4 vCPU of AMD EPYC); elsewhere (browsers, Workers, Deno)
   WebCrypto's Ed25519 does, about 65 µs with its asynchronous hop. Neither
   is a static import, so the browser bundle carries no node: module.

   An rtsk_ secret is a private key: every call that authorizes with the
   API key goes through `authorize` (signs) or `bearerKey` (refuses it), so
   it is never sent, whatever the call. */


